WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.221
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.521 --> 00:00:09.965
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.265 --> 00:00:16.157
Unit eighteen: Grant Proposals. Today you will set formal conditions with Should… and Were… to.

4
00:00:16.457 --> 00:00:18.935
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:23.075
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.917 --> 00:00:25.231
Funding request.

7
00:00:26.217 --> 00:00:30.573
Funding request means the amount of money that a proposal asks a funder to give.

8
00:00:31.183 --> 00:00:33.903
The funding request totals fifty thousand dollars.

9
00:00:34.517 --> 00:00:37.622
Your turn. Say: Funding request.

10
00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:41.816
Funding request.

11
00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:43.876
Project narrative.

12
00:00:44.883 --> 00:00:48.961
Project narrative means the main text that explains a project's aims and plan.

13
00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:51.898
The project narrative runs to ten pages.

14
00:00:52.483 --> 00:00:55.948
Your turn. Say: Project narrative.

15
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.115
Project narrative.

16
00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:02.455
Budget justification.

17
00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:07.609
Budget justification means an explanation of why each cost is needed.

18
00:01:08.217 --> 00:01:11.191
The budget justification explains every salary.

19
00:01:11.783 --> 00:01:15.155
Your turn. Say: Budget justification.

20
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.547
Budget justification.

21
00:01:20.283 --> 00:01:21.482
Grant reviewer.

22
00:01:22.483 --> 00:01:26.700
Grant reviewer means an expert who judges proposals and recommends funding.

23
00:01:27.317 --> 00:01:29.873
The grant reviewer praised the clear timeline.

24
00:01:30.483 --> 00:01:33.414
Your turn. Say: Grant reviewer.

25
00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:37.384
Grant reviewer.

26
00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:39.441
Impact statement.

27
00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:44.760
Impact statement means a section that explains the benefits the project will bring.

28
00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:47.791
The impact statement promised cleaner water.

29
00:01:48.383 --> 00:01:51.477
Your turn. Say: Impact statement.

30
00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:56.147
Impact statement.

31
00:01:56.883 --> 00:01:57.942
Feasibility.

32
00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:02.703
Feasibility means how realistic it is that the plan can be carried out.

33
00:02:03.317 --> 00:02:06.082
Feasibility depends on the team and the schedule.

34
00:02:06.683 --> 00:02:09.591
Your turn. Say: Feasibility.

35
00:02:12.433 --> 00:02:13.574
Feasibility.

36
00:02:14.317 --> 00:02:15.666
Proposal deadline.

37
00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:20.519
Proposal deadline means the last date on which a proposal may be sent.

38
00:02:21.117 --> 00:02:23.534
The proposal deadline is the first of May.

39
00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:26.976
Your turn. Say: Proposal deadline.

40
00:02:29.833 --> 00:02:31.450
Proposal deadline.

41
00:02:32.183 --> 00:02:33.951
Successful application.

42
00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:39.132
Successful application means a request that is approved and receives funding.

43
00:02:39.717 --> 00:02:42.308
A successful application needs a clear aim.

44
00:02:42.917 --> 00:02:46.254
Your turn. Say: Successful application.

45
00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:51.227
Successful application.

46
00:02:52.033 --> 00:02:56.044
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:56.883 --> 00:03:02.926
Visal, a proposal says 'Should the funding be approved, we will begin in June.' Why not 'If the funding is approved'?

48
00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:18.224
Should + subject + base verb is a formal, slightly less likely condition. It replaces if… should. It sounds professional and polite, and it is common in business and academic writing.

49
00:03:19.317 --> 00:03:21.258
And 'Were the project to succeed'?

50
00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:34.720
Were + subject + to + base verb imagines a possible future more formally: Were the project to succeed, it could be repeated elsewhere. It can replace if… were to.

51
00:03:35.817 --> 00:03:37.735
How is the result clause formed?

52
00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:51.545
Use will, would, could or can: Should you need more detail, please contact us. Do not use a present verb after Should in the result unless it is a request or command.

53
00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:54.316
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:59.367
Grammar focus: Formal conditionals: Should… and Were… to.

55
00:03:59.667 --> 00:04:00.947
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:04.625
Should the funding be approved, we will begin work in June.

57
00:04:05.517 --> 00:04:09.513
Were the project to succeed, it could be repeated in other provinces.

58
00:04:10.417 --> 00:04:13.496
Should you need more detail, please contact the team.

59
00:04:14.433 --> 00:04:25.030
Should + subject + base verb = formal if… should. Were + subject + to + base verb = formal if… were to. The result clause uses will, would, could or an imperative.

60
00:04:25.783 --> 00:04:29.945
Be careful! Don't say: Should the plan fails, we will stop.

61
00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:34.526
Say: Should the plan fail, we will stop.

62
00:04:34.826 --> 00:04:37.395
Use the base verb after Should + subject.

63
00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:40.806
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.007
Asking for Funding.

65
00:04:43.307 --> 00:04:48.035
A grant proposal explains a project in a clear narrative and ends with a funding request.

66
00:04:49.033 --> 00:04:52.809
Should the reviewer have questions, the budget justification must answer them.

67
00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:58.017
Were the project to succeed, the impact statement would show who benefits and how.

68
00:04:59.033 --> 00:05:05.828
Feasibility is judged carefully, because reviewers will reject a plan that cannot be completed before the proposal deadline.

69
00:05:06.833 --> 00:05:11.538
A successful application is clear, realistic and honest about its risks.

70
00:05:12.633 --> 00:05:16.473
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:17.317 --> 00:05:19.781
blank the plan fail, we will try again.

72
00:05:20.081 --> 00:05:23.235
A, Should. B, If should. C, Would.

73
00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:28.637
The answer is A: Should.

74
00:05:29.783 --> 00:05:34.093
A section that explains the benefits a project will bring is the blank statement.

75
00:05:34.393 --> 00:05:37.722
A, budget. B, impact. C, funding.

76
00:05:41.267 --> 00:05:43.057
The answer is B: impact.

77
00:05:44.217 --> 00:05:45.856
Which sentence is correct?

78
00:05:46.156 --> 00:05:53.281
A, Were the team to win, they would celebrate.. B, Were the team win, they would celebrate.. C, Was the team to win, they would celebrate..

79
00:05:56.833 --> 00:06:00.075
The answer is A: Were the team to win, they would celebrate..

80
00:06:01.217 --> 00:06:04.749
How realistic it is that a plan can be carried out is its blank.

81
00:06:05.049 --> 00:06:09.422
A, narrative. B, justification. C, feasibility.

82
00:06:12.967 --> 00:06:15.256
The answer is C: feasibility.

83
00:06:16.417 --> 00:06:19.124
According to the reading, what will reviewers reject?

84
00:06:19.424 --> 00:06:24.831
A, a plan that cannot be completed. B, a short title. C, a polite tone.

85
00:06:28.367 --> 00:06:31.632
The answer is A: a plan that cannot be completed.

86
00:06:32.833 --> 00:06:35.904
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:40.642
What makes a grant proposal's 'impact statement' convincing to a reviewer?

88
00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:50.950
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:51.250 --> 00:07:17.671
An impact statement is convincing when it is specific, realistic and linked to evidence. A reviewer wants to know who will benefit, how many people will be reached and how success will be measured. Were the claims too broad, the reviewer would doubt them, so clear numbers, a believable plan and honest limits make the statement stronger.

90
00:07:17.971 --> 00:07:19.761
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:20.650 --> 00:07:26.251
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and formal conditionals: should… and were… to.

92
00:07:26.551 --> 00:07:32.164
Today's value is strategic vision. See the bigger picture, and plan for where you want to be.

93
00:07:32.464 --> 00:07:35.151
See you in Unit nineteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:35.451 --> 00:07:38.974
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

